{
  "version": "herdr-core 0.1.13 on hc-lab, Debian 13, Linux 6.18.54 x86_64 (CLK_TCK 100), 2026-11-03, signed in: Codex CLI 0.165.0 (npm) with its app-server daemon 0.160.0 and Grok Build 1.0.70 on the owner's accounts, OpenCode 2.2.2, Pi 1.4.0 and Oh My Pi 18.13.3 on bun 1.4.4 on the owner's model endpoint, no Host Helper extension; real HOME /data/home, one prompt each",
  "method": "Live, one Terminal per program started through terminal.run in Heeler's POSIX launch form, one sample of each scene. Rows are a watcher's 0.2 s samples of the Terminal's process and its descendants by parent: comm, field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat, argv, HC_SCOPE alone of the environment (its hex a counter here), and open files, trimmed to session files, locks and the session store. The watcher read open files only under the programs' folders, so /dev/pts/0, the terminal each Oh My Pi Terminal got, comes from the run's record. A scene's record is the core's reply to terminal.run, with the foreground its events named and the argv sampled. File contents are rebuilt from the values the run's record quotes: rollouts keep their first line's identifying fields, with the two creator ids <scrubbed>; active_sessions.json takes the shape and size the watcher saw, its opened_at to the millisecond; omp-after-reply's breadcrumb is the one its process left once the file appeared. omp-fresh-breadcrumb's is as read. Every session and thread id is a synthetic one of the same form, and the run folder is /data/home/user, as the capture script wrote it.",
  "observed": "Codex: the npm launcher stays as the Terminal's process; the native child holds the thread-writer lock once the folder is trusted and the rollout from the first prompt; the MCP servers the box's configuration starts sit under it with no HC_SCOPE. Default mode started the box's older 0.160.0 app-server daemon, which carries the Terminal's HC_SCOPE and holds its lock and rollout; it ended with the Terminal. Grok Build: pid_start is field 22 of /proc/<pid>/stat and the HC_SCOPE prefix; no leader. OpenCode: its service process holds opencode.db, and the session row took the given id at the first prompt. Pi: it retitles itself to pi with argv pi, and no open file, lock or socket names its session file. Oh My Pi: after the reply the file existed and the breadcrumb had dropped fresh, but the process never opened the file before it exited 2.5 minutes later, its title model having returned none; it held only an abstract socket @omp-file-lock-<hash>, which a read of open files does not name. The same day's Chat stage saw such a file opened 8.6 s after a send, in a run whose title was generated, so the open may follow the title model (one sample each way).",
